    {
      "id": "43kmMRTNaok2y0nlbpajR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -5482.718301143096,
      "y": 1131.4045115177828,
      "width": 2574.36673358407,
      "height": 2057.7833069304534,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "e11RJXSkJ0IKqyNC85gPi"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b58",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 705701056,
      "version": 2470,
      "versionNonce": 1582273852,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788851934283,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q3: read/watch path scaling\n\nWhy its important to optimize read operation?\nBecause youtube is read heavy\n\n\nscenario: User from US uploads a video and a user from India watches \nit explain what happens?\n\n1. What happens step by step from \"user clicks play\" to \"video starts streaming\" — where does the request \ngo first, and how does it find the video fast? \n\n- user clicks play \n- client browser requests the vidoe's manifest file from the nearest CDN edge server\n- manfiest file is like an index of indexes. Pointing to the various resolution and the chunks\n- request it from the nearest CDN edge\n- if cache hit then server directly therefore resulting in low latency\n\n\n2. What happens if the video is not yet cached in the India CDN edge (cache miss) — \nwhat's the fallback path? \ncache miss - CDN server doesn't have the original video\n- take a pull from original blob storage\n- the first users pays a latency penalty after that all user uses the cached version\n\n\n3. How does the client actually decide which resolution to request, and how does it switch mid-playback if network conditions change\n (e.g., user goes from WiFi to weak mobile data)?\n\nABR = adaptive bitrate streaming\n\nbased on the clients network strength decide which resolution to server \nwithout starting the playback from the beginning\n\n- client monitors it download throughput (how fast the recent chunks arrive)\n- each chunk is 2-10 seconds long\n- before requesting the ABR algorithm computes which resolution to send next\n- because chunks are independent file per resolutions switching is seamless \nno restart just the next chunk come from a differenet quality tier\n\n4.  how would you handle the \"view count\" update for this video without hammering your database on every single play?\n- the count are not updated every time a new viewer arrives. For viral/trending videos it can hammer the DB\nmultiple times for the same row cause serious contention\n- the count is pushed to a async message queue\n- after sometime in one go the count is updated\n- therefore two viewer can see different view count. Since it can be stale. Because \navailability is emphaise over consistency.",
      "fontSize": 35.02609884136942,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q3: read/watch path scaling\n\nWhy its important to optimize read operation?\nBecause youtube is read heavy\n\n\nscenario: User from US uploads a video and a user from India watches \nit explain what happens?\n\n1. What happens step by step from \"user clicks play\" to \"video starts streaming\" — where does the request \ngo first, and how does it find the video fast? \n\n- user clicks play \n- client browser requests the vidoe's manifest file from the nearest CDN edge server\n- manfiest file is like an index of indexes. Pointing to the various resolution and the chunks\n- request it from the nearest CDN edge\n- if cache hit then server directly therefore resulting in low latency\n\n\n2. What happens if the video is not yet cached in the India CDN edge (cache miss) — \nwhat's the fallback path? \ncache miss - CDN server doesn't have the original video\n- take a pull from original blob storage\n- the first users pays a latency penalty after that all user uses the cached version\n\n\n3. How does the client actually decide which resolution to request, and how does it switch mid-playback if network conditions change\n (e.g., user goes from WiFi to weak mobile data)?\n\nABR = adaptive bitrate streaming\n\nbased on the clients network strength decide which resolution to server \nwithout starting the playback from the beginning\n\n- client monitors it download throughput (how fast the recent chunks arrive)\n- each chunk is 2-10 seconds long\n- before requesting the ABR algorithm computes which resolution to send next\n- because chunks are independent file per resolutions switching is seamless \nno restart just the next chunk come from a differenet quality tier\n\n4.  how would you handle the \"view count\" update for this video without hammering your database on every single play?\n- the count are not updated every time a new viewer arrives. For viral/trending videos it can hammer the DB\nmultiple times for the same row cause serious contention\n- the count is pushed to a async message queue\n- after sometime in one go the count is updated\n- therefore two viewer can see different view count. Since it can be stale. Because \navailability is emphaise over consistency.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "q-gz9EHH8JSo8_adJ4tGV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1783.523528032735,
      "y": -946.3814705369308,
      "width": 1680.7388916015625,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5A",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1186380640,
      "version": 1352,
      "versionNonce": 537685124,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788851934283,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For the core videos table (title, description, owner, duration, upload date, status, blob URLs per resolution) — would you use SQL or NoSQL? \nJustify with the actual reasoning (not just \"it has relationships\").\n\nSQL suits best for this requirement \n\n1. Strong consistency: a video belongs only to one user/owner. If the channel/user is deleted all the videos associated with \nthe channel/user will also be cascade and be deleted. Having strong consistency ACID properties of SQL DB is much more \neffcient and reliable\n\n2. structured schema\neach video has the same details such as title, description, tags etc. Most of those details are mandatory. \nSQL DB offer this.\n\n3. write operation of video are rare\nThe title, description etc, are occasionally updated.\n\n\n\nComments on a video can be nested (replies to replies). How would you model comment data — SQL with a parent_comment_id foreign key, or a NoSQL document structure? \nWhat breaks at scale with each approach?\n\n\n                                    reading                                         writing\n\n                                                                        You have to search for the subdocument \n                         reading is easy. You pick the            then write inside it\n                         document or the subdocument to\n                         get all the threads\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For the core videos table (title, description, owner, duration, upload date, status, blob URLs per resolution) — would you use SQL or NoSQL? \nJustify with the actual reasoning (not just \"it has relationships\").\n\nSQL suits best for this requirement \n\n1. Strong consistency: a video belongs only to one user/owner. If the channel/user is deleted all the videos associated with \nthe channel/user will also be cascade and be deleted. Having strong consistency ACID properties of SQL DB is much more \neffcient and reliable\n\n2. structured schema\neach video has the same details such as title, description, tags etc. Most of those details are mandatory. \nSQL DB offer this.\n\n3. write operation of video are rare\nThe title, description etc, are occasionally updated.\n\n\n\nComments on a video can be nested (replies to replies). How would you model comment data — SQL with a parent_comment_id foreign key, or a NoSQL document structure? \nWhat breaks at scale with each approach?\n\n\n                                    reading                                         writing\n\n                                                                        You have to search for the subdocument \n                         reading is easy. You pick the            then write inside it\n                         document or the subdocument to\n                         get all the threads\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
