      "text": "\nread:write ratio\n\nYoutube is read heavy application",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\nread:write ratio\n\nYoutube is read heavy application",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "_vGpvEn1RBo62Zddyyb8U",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7930.03258453006,
      "y": 2925.27346023074,
      "width": 1588.4951171875,
      "height": 1645,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBz",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1803885833,
      "version": 1850,
      "versionNonce": 979086724,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788851934283,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "what possible can go wrong in transcoding process?\n\nstep 1: breaking into chunks\n\n\nproblems\ncutting a chunk in the middle of delta frame sequence\nas chunk has no reference point to decode. You get broken/glitchy\nvider\n\nsolutions\nbefore splitting run ffprobe to list where all the keyframes are. Only cut at those points\nmost tools default to keyframes every 2-4 seconds\n\nstep 2 transcoding each chunk\n\nproblems\n1. a transcoder responsible for transcoding a chunk goes down(server restart, spot instance got reclaimed)\n   1.1 missing chunk. Reassembly either fails or skip silently\n   1.2 retry system again send the chunk for transcoding because the inital worker is slow\n       creates a duplicate chunk\n\n2. chunk is corrupted and keeps crashing the transcoding worker so it retries indefinitely\n\n3. transcoding worker are of different types handles different bitrates and resolutions so they can mix up together\n\nsolution\n\n1. store the status of each chunk (pending,failed,done) in DB so each transcoding worker\nknows wheather to pick up the new chunk or not. If status is failed then give to a \ndifferent worker\n\n2. each chunk will have retry limit\n\n3. before the transcoding process define the bitrate and resolution or group similar transcoder\ntogether so that there chunks are grouped and sent to reassembly\n\n\nstep 3 reassembly \n\nproblem \nalmost all chunks are transcoded but few pending. Reassembly can build the new transcoded file\nwith missing chunks\n\nsolution\nreassembly process starts only when all chunks are available\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "what possible can go wrong in transcoding process?\n\nstep 1: breaking into chunks\n\n\nproblems\ncutting a chunk in the middle of delta frame sequence\nas chunk has no reference point to decode. You get broken/glitchy\nvider\n\nsolutions\nbefore splitting run ffprobe to list where all the keyframes are. Only cut at those points\nmost tools default to keyframes every 2-4 seconds\n\nstep 2 transcoding each chunk\n\nproblems\n1. a transcoder responsible for transcoding a chunk goes down(server restart, spot instance got reclaimed)\n   1.1 missing chunk. Reassembly either fails or skip silently\n   1.2 retry system again send the chunk for transcoding because the inital worker is slow\n       creates a duplicate chunk\n\n2. chunk is corrupted and keeps crashing the transcoding worker so it retries indefinitely\n\n3. transcoding worker are of different types handles different bitrates and resolutions so they can mix up together\n\nsolution\n\n1. store the status of each chunk (pending,failed,done) in DB so each transcoding worker\nknows wheather to pick up the new chunk or not. If status is failed then give to a \ndifferent worker\n\n2. each chunk will have retry limit\n\n3. before the transcoding process define the bitrate and resolution or group similar transcoder\ntogether so that there chunks are grouped and sent to reassembly\n\n\nstep 3 reassembly \n\nproblem \nalmost all chunks are transcoded but few pending. Reassembly can build the new transcoded file\nwith missing chunks\n\nsolution\nreassembly process starts only when all chunks are available\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "bjwjABQi9NIgQ73cIl8eP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7917.615710185785,
      "y": 4637.738881898209,
      "width": 1594.487060546875,
      "height": 1785,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bC0",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 222580582,
      "version": 2420,
      "versionNonce": 1822612668,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788851934283,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "explain how large video files are uploaded to youtube(multi-part upload)?\n\n1. client sends the video along with the metadata to the server\n2. server verifies the request. If the request valid it creates an upload session and returns a pre-signed URL\n   (resumable upload session URL) pointing directly at the BLOB storage system.\n3. BLOB storage itself handles chunk assembly, resumablitity tracking and integrity checks.\n4. once the video is successfully uploaded BLOB storage/client notifies the server via a lightweight webhook/event\n   that the video is successfully uploaded.\n\n\nHow does BLOB storage enforce integrity during the upload process?\n\n1. the server enforces a max total size at session creation time\n2. cummulative stiched chunk size should not exceed max total size at session creation time\n3. Byte ranges and offsets\n\n    client sends Content-Range header on the incoming request \n    eg: \"this chunk is bytes 40000000000–40000005000\"\n    but the server keeps track of upload session state\n    e.g., \"for session X, I have received and stitched exactly 104,857,600 bytes (100MB), contiguous from offset 0.\"\n\n4. rate limits on max byte that a client send. No uploads over X GB in an hour or day or week etc\n5. verify the clients check sum\n6. check the file type and content\n   The server should only accept certain file extension and also check and sanitize the bytes it receives\n    because a malicious user can rename an executable to video.mp4 and set Content-Type: video/mp4\n\n7. duplicate chunks\n   The client can send(accidentally or maliciously) the same chunk multiple times\n8. verify chunks are send for the correct session\n\n\nWhy chunk client-side instead of just streaming the whole file in one request?\n\n1. resumability\n   If you stream one entire file and it dies at 40GB. You can't resume from 40GB inside \n   a singly HTTP request.\n   discreate chunks with acknowledged offsets let you resume precisely.\n\n2. size limit on request and timeouts on long-lived connection\n   Many load balancers/proxies have max limit on request body size\n\n3. retry granularity\n   Retrying a 5MB chunk is more cheaper and faster than 50GB file\n\n4. parallelism \n   Many chunks can be uplaoded in parallel together\n\n5. progress reporting\n   X% progess is more accuate since you know how many bytes/chunks have been acknowleded\n    ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "explain how large video files are uploaded to youtube(multi-part upload)?\n\n1. client sends the video along with the metadata to the server\n2. server verifies the request. If the request valid it creates an upload session and returns a pre-signed URL\n   (resumable upload session URL) pointing directly at the BLOB storage system.\n3. BLOB storage itself handles chunk assembly, resumablitity tracking and integrity checks.\n4. once the video is successfully uploaded BLOB storage/client notifies the server via a lightweight webhook/event\n   that the video is successfully uploaded.\n\n\nHow does BLOB storage enforce integrity during the upload process?\n\n1. the server enforces a max total size at session creation time\n2. cummulative stiched chunk size should not exceed max total size at session creation time\n3. Byte ranges and offsets\n\n    client sends Content-Range header on the incoming request \n    eg: \"this chunk is bytes 40000000000–40000005000\"\n    but the server keeps track of upload session state\n    e.g., \"for session X, I have received and stitched exactly 104,857,600 bytes (100MB), contiguous from offset 0.\"\n\n4. rate limits on max byte that a client send. No uploads over X GB in an hour or day or week etc\n5. verify the clients check sum\n6. check the file type and content\n   The server should only accept certain file extension and also check and sanitize the bytes it receives\n    because a malicious user can rename an executable to video.mp4 and set Content-Type: video/mp4\n\n7. duplicate chunks\n   The client can send(accidentally or maliciously) the same chunk multiple times\n8. verify chunks are send for the correct session\n\n\nWhy chunk client-side instead of just streaming the whole file in one request?\n\n1. resumability\n   If you stream one entire file and it dies at 40GB. You can't resume from 40GB inside \n   a singly HTTP request.\n   discreate chunks with acknowledged offsets let you resume precisely.\n\n2. size limit on request and timeouts on long-lived connection\n   Many load balancers/proxies have max limit on request body size\n\n3. retry granularity\n   Retrying a 5MB chunk is more cheaper and faster than 50GB file\n\n4. parallelism \n   Many chunks can be uplaoded in parallel together\n\n5. progress reporting\n   X% progess is more accuate since you know how many bytes/chunks have been acknowleded\n    ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
